/// Chapter 6 of the guide, written from md-docs/learn/06-elements.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page Learn06Elements(path: "/learn/06-elements", title: "Elements", description: "The element call shape: positional, named props, flags and enum cases, the block and its order, attributes, parts, and the families of built-ins.", layout: DocsShell("Elements", kind: .tutorial, chapter: t("nav.group.language"), blurb: "Everything on screen is an element, and every element is written the same way: a name, one positional value, named props, flags and a block.", source: "md-docs/learn/06-elements.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "the-call-shape", label: "The call shape"), Anchor(id: "props-cases-and-flags", label: "Props, cases and flags"), Anchor(id: "attributes", label: "Attributes"), Anchor(id: "parts", label: "Parts"), Anchor(id: "text-and-headings", label: "Text and headings"), Anchor(id: "layout-elements", label: "Layout elements"), Anchor(id: "the-families-of-built-ins", label: "The families of built-ins"), Anchor(id: "what-an-element-renders", label: "What an element renders"), Anchor(id: "what-the-compiler-checks-on-every-element", label: "What the compiler checks on every element"), Anchor(id: "quick-reference", label: "Quick reference"), Anchor(id: "common-mistakes", label: "Common mistakes")], learned: ["Read and write any element call: `Name(value, prop: value).flag \{ block \}`", "Pick an enum prop's case by name (`gap: .md`) or as a flag (`.primary`)", "Put HTML attributes such as `aria-*`, `data-*` and `id` on an element", "Place parts inside their owner: `Card.Header`, `Table.Row`", "Know which built-in family to reach for, and what the compiler checks on each call"], prevTo: "/learn/05-pages-and-routing", prevLabel: t("ch.05"), nextTo: "/learn/07-state-and-reactivity", nextLabel: t("ch.07"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter covers WebFluent's elements: everything a page shows, whether a built-in such as `Button` or a component you declare. Every element is written in the same shape, so once you can read one call you can read them all.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- the shape of an element call, and the order of its block\n- props, enum cases and flags, and the props every element takes\n- HTML attributes, and which element of a built-in receives them\n- parts, such as `Card.Header`\n- the text, heading and layout elements\n- the families of built-ins, the HTML they render, and what the compiler checks on every element")

        Heading("The call shape", id: "the-call-shape").h2

        Markdown("Every element call has the same five pieces, each optional except the name:")

        CodeBlock(#"Name(positional, prop: value, prop: value).flag.flag { block }"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("- **`Name`** is PascalCase: a built-in (`Card`), a part of one (`Card.Header`), or a component of the project (`UserCard`, `Panel.Footer`).\n- **One positional argument** comes first, unnamed. It is the element's main value: a button's label, a heading's text, an icon's name. You pass every other value by name.\n- **Named props**, `name: value`, come in any order, and the value may be any expression.\n- **Flags**, `.name`, are written tight against the closing parenthesis, or against the name when there are no parentheses: `Button(\"Save\").primary.lg`, `Spacer.sm`, `Card.elevated \{ … \}`. An element or a component called with no arguments needs no parentheses: `Spinner` and `Spinner()` are the same call, and this book writes the shorter one.\n- **The block**, `\{ … \}`, holds its contents in a fixed order: `style \{ \}`, then `transition \{ \}`, then `on … \{ \}` handlers, then slot fills, then children.\n\nThis button uses all five pieces, with its block in that order:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "A save button, fully dressed.") {
    Heading("Editor").h1
    Button("Save", disabled: false).primary.lg {
        style { min-width: 120px }
        transition { background: 150ms ease-out }
        on click { log("saved") }
        Icon("check")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Callout("**Coming from JSX or a Vue template?** There are no tags and no string attributes: `<Button variant=\"primary\" size=\"lg\">Save</Button>` is `Button(\"Save\").primary.lg`. A prop's value is an expression, never a string to be parsed, and children go in the block. See [Coming from React](/coming-from/react#the-map).", kind: .note)

        Heading("Props, cases and flags", id: "props-cases-and-flags").h2

        Markdown("Each prop has a type the compiler knows: `String`, `Number`, `Bool`, a state binding, a path, or an **enum**, which is a fixed set of named cases. You pass an enum prop a case, written with a leading dot:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Toolbar(path: "/", title: "Toolbar", description: "Two labels, spaced apart.") {
    Heading("Toolbar").h1
    Row(gap: .md, align: .center, justify: .between) {
        Text("Drafts")
        Text("3 unsent")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A **flag** is a short way to write one of two things: a `Bool` prop set to `true`, or a case of one of the element's enum props. Each comment shows the long spelling of the flags on its line:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Search(path: "/", title: "Search", description: "A search box and two buttons.") {
    state term = ""
    Heading("Search").h1
    Button("Delete").danger                                // tone: .danger
    Button("Continue").full.outlined                       // width: .full, outlined: true
    Input(bind: term, label: "Search").search.required     // type: .search, required: true
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The two spellings mean the same thing. `.md` on a `Row` means `gap: .md`, because `gap` is the only prop of `Row` with an `md` case. A flag follows these rules:\n\n- When two enum props of an element share a case name, the flag is ambiguous. The compiler reports `E103` and asks you to name the prop.\n- A flag the element does not declare is error `E103`, and the message lists the flags the element takes. There is no shared vocabulary of modifiers to guess from.\n- A `Bool` prop may take a condition instead of a flag: `Button(\"Light\", outlined: theme != \"light\")`.")

        Heading("Universal props", id: "universal-props").h3

        Markdown("Every element also takes the *universal props*. The reference does not repeat them on each element:")

        Table(caption: "Universal props") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Prop").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                    Table.Cell("Read more").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`class:`") }
                    Table.Cell("Adds classes from your own stylesheets. It adds; it never replaces")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Styling](/learn/13-styling#classes-and-your-own-stylesheet)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ref:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Names a handle on the element: `box.focus()`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[State and reactivity](/learn/07-state-and-reactivity#element-handles)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`mount:`, `update:`, `cleanup:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Hands the element to a script each time it is made, again when what `update` reads changes, and takes it back when it leaves") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Hand an element to a script](/recipes/hand-an-element-to-a-script)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`animate:` or a flag (`.fadeIn`)") }
                    Table.Cell("The animation it enters with")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Animate in and out](/recipes/animate-in-and-out#animate-an-element-in)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`exit:`") }
                    Table.Cell("The animation it leaves with")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Animate in and out](/recipes/animate-in-and-out#animate-an-element-out)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`delay:`, `duration:`, `speed:` (`.fast`, `.slow`), `easing:`") }
                    Table.Cell("How the animation is timed")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Animate in and out](/recipes/animate-in-and-out), [Motion defaults](/recipes/motion-defaults)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`stagger:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Inside a `for`, each item's delay grows by this much") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Animate a list](/recipes/animate-a-list)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`on:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("When the animation plays: `.mount` (the default) or `.enterView`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Animate in and out](/recipes/animate-in-and-out#play-an-animation-when-it-is-scrolled-to)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`shared:`") }
                    Table.Cell("A name the element keeps across a route change")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Shared element](/recipes/shared-element)") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`count:`") }
                    Table.Cell("A number in its text counts up or down to its new value")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("[Count a number](/recipes/count-a-number)") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The [components reference](/reference) lists every element's own props.")

        Heading("Attributes", id: "attributes").h2

        Markdown("A named argument that is not a prop becomes an HTML attribute. These families are declared, so they draw no warning:\n\n- the global attributes: `id`, `title`, `role`, `tabindex`, `lang`, `dir`, `hidden`;\n- `aria-*` and `data-*`;\n- the attributes of the element's kind: input, anchor, media, form and table attributes on the elements that take them.\n\nAny other name is still written to the element, with warning `C04`. You write a hyphenated name as it is:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Signup(path: "/", title: "Sign up", description: "A name field and a menu button.") {
    state name = ""
    Heading("Sign up").h1
    Button("More", aria-expanded: "false", aria-controls: "panel", data-track: "more")
    Input(bind: name, label: "Name", id: "name", autocomplete: "name", maxlength: 40)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Attribute values", id: "attribute-values").h3

        Markdown("An attribute's value follows these rules:\n\n- A value that reads state is kept in step with it.\n- `true` is written as the word: `data-open: true` renders `data-open=\"true\"`, so the selector `[data-open=\"true\"]` matches it.\n- A boolean attribute such as `hidden` or `disabled` is either present or absent.\n- `false` removes an attribute, except an `aria-*` one, which keeps the string `\"false\"` because it is a real ARIA state.\n\nThe same rules apply to an attribute you write on a call of your own component, which passes it to the component's root.")

        Heading("Where a built-in puts them", id: "where-a-built-in-puts-them").h3

        Markdown("A built-in places your attributes and `on` handlers on the element it draws:\n\n- On its root, for most built-ins: the scrolling box of a `VirtualList`, the handle of a `Splitter`, the box of a `Tabs` or a `Carousel`, the panel of a `Sidebar`, the `<nav>` of a `Breadcrumb`, the `<dialog>` of a `Modal`.\n- On its input, for a control drawn inside a box (`Switch`, `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `Slider`, `DatePicker`, `FileUpload`). The input is the control, and its own handlers are already there. `hidden`, `lang` and `dir` belong to the whole control, so they stay on its box.\n\nA `role:` or `aria-label:` you write takes the place of the one the built-in gives itself: a `Splitter`'s `separator`, a `Switch`'s `switch`, a `Breadcrumb`'s name. A `Sidebar`'s `id:` is the one its toggle points at.\n\nThis list box puts `role`, `tabindex` and `aria-activedescendant` on the `VirtualList`'s scrolling box, and a `role` and `id` on each row:")

        CodeBlock(#"page References(path: "/", title: "References", description: "A list box of references.") {
    state refs = [{ id: "a" }, { id: "b" }, { id: "c" }]
    state current = 0
    Heading("References").h1
    VirtualList(rowHeight: 24, label: "References", role: "listbox", tabindex: "0", aria-activedescendant: "ref-{current}") {
        style { height: 240px }
        on keydown(e) { if e.key == "ArrowDown" { current = current + 1 } }
        for r in refs by r.id { Text(r.id, id: "ref-{r.id}", role: "option") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The pre-rendered page and the template engine write the same attributes on the same elements, as far as the build knows their values.")

        Heading("Parts", id: "parts").h2

        Markdown("Some built-ins are made of parts. You write a part under its owner's name, inside its owner:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Regions(path: "/", title: "Regions", description: "A card, a table, a select and tabs.") {
    state region = "fra1"
    Heading("Regions").h1
    Card.elevated {
        Card.Header { Heading("Frankfurt").h2 }
        Card.Body { Text("Our main region.") }
        Card.Footer { Button("Open").primary }
    }
    Table(caption: "Regions") {
        Table.Head { Table.Row { Table.Cell("Region").header  Table.Cell("Latency").header } }
        Table.Body { Table.Row { Table.Cell("fra1")  Table.Cell("34 ms") } }
    }
    Select(bind: region, label: "Region") {
        Select.Option("Frankfurt", value: "fra1")
        Select.Option("Iowa", value: "iad1")
    }
    Tabs {
        Tabs.Page("Summary") { Text("Two regions.") }
        Tabs.Page("Details") { Text("fra1 and iad1.") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A part belongs inside its owner. `Card.Body` outside a `Card` is error `C03`. Your own components may declare parts too ([Components](/learn/10-components)).\n\nA `Tabs` keeps the open tab in a state of yours with `bind:`, holding the open page's `value:`. Only the open tab is in the Tab order; the arrow keys move between tabs, the other way round in a right-to-left page:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Account(path: "/account", title: "Account", description: "Your profile and billing.") {
    state tab = "profile"
    Heading("Account").h1
    Tabs(bind: tab, label: "Account") {
        Tabs.Page("Profile", value: "profile") { Text("Your name and photo.") }
        Tabs.Page("Billing", value: "billing") { Text("Your plan and invoices.") }
    }
    Text("Open: {tab}")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Any element of your own can move the same way: `roving: .x` makes a row one stop for Tab, with the arrow keys inside it ([Events](/learn/08-events#moving-through-a-group-with-the-arrow-keys)).")

        Heading("Text and headings", id: "text-and-headings").h2

        Markdown("The typography elements cover inline text, headings, paragraphs, code, quotations and Markdown:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Article(path: "/", title: "Article", description: "Every kind of text.") {
    state count = 3
    Heading("Release notes").h1                // a real <h1>; .h2 is the default
    Text("{count} changes").bold.muted         // a run of text
    Paragraph("Prose, as a <p>.")
    Code("let x = 1").block
    Blockquote("A quotation.")
    Markdown("**Bold** and a [link](/docs)")   // Markdown, rendered as HTML
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The compiler checks the outline the headings form: a page needs an `h1` (`A12`), and a heading level may not be skipped, such as `h2` straight to `h4` (`A11`).\n\n[Render Markdown](/recipes/render-markdown) shows what `Markdown` draws, and how to show text somebody else wrote.")

        Heading("Layout elements", id: "layout-elements").h2

        Markdown("The layout elements arrange what is inside them. Each comment says what the element is:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Dashboard(path: "/", title: "Dashboard", description: "Every layout element at once.") {
    Header { Heading("Dashboard").h1 }
    Container {                                  // centred, with a max width
        Row(gap: .md, align: .center) {          // horizontal flex
            Column(span: 8) { Text("main") }     // a child of a 12-column grid
            Column(span: 4) { Text("aside") }
        }
        Grid(columns: 3, gap: .lg) { Card { Text("a") }  Card { Text("b") }  Card { Text("c") } }
        Stack(gap: .sm) { Text("one")  Text("two") }   // vertical flex
        Spacer.lg
        Divider
    }
    Section { Text("another region") }
    Footer { Text("the last region") }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`Header`, `Section` and `Footer` render real landmarks: `<header>`, `<section>` and `<footer>`. Use them for the page's regions, so a screen reader and a search engine can follow the page's outline. Inside a `Document`, `Header` and `Footer` mean something else: they are drawn in the margin of every printed page ([PDF and slides](/learn/17-pdf-and-slides#set-the-paper-headers-and-footers)).")

        Heading("The families of built-ins", id: "the-families-of-built-ins").h2

        Markdown("The built-ins fall into these families:")

        Table(caption: "The families of built-ins") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Family").header
                    Table.Cell("Elements").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Layout")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`AppShell`, `Container`, `Row`, `Column`, `Grid`, `Stack`, `Spacer`, `Divider`, `Header`, `Section`, `Footer`, `VirtualList`, `Splitter`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Navigation")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Navbar`, `Sidebar`, `Link`, `Tabs`, `Breadcrumb`, `Menu`, `OnThisPage`, `Pagination`, `Stepper`, `CommandPalette`, `NavigationMenu`, `Menubar`, `ContextMenu`, `BottomNavigation`, `PrevNext`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Data display")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Card`, `Table`, `DataTable`, `Tree`, `List`, `Badge`, `Tag`, `Avatar`, `Tooltip` ([Lists of data](/learn/15-data#lists-of-data-a-table-a-tree-a-search))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Content")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Callout`, `Kbd`, `EmptyState`, `Stat`, `CodeBlock`, `Chip`, `Accordion`, `VisuallyHidden` ([below](#content-a-note-a-key-a-figure))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Forms")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Form`, `Input`, `Textarea`, `Select`, `Checkbox`, `Radio`, `Switch`, `Slider`, `DatePicker`, `FileUpload`, `Field`, `Fieldset`, `InputGroup`, `RadioGroup`, `NumberInput`, `Calendar`, `Listbox`, `Combobox`, `CheckboxGroup`, `InputOTP`, `TagInput`, `Rating`, `DateRangePicker` ([Forms](/learn/14-forms))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Feedback")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Alert`, `Toast`, `Modal`, `Dialog`, `Popover`, `Spinner`, `Progress`, `Skeleton` ([Events](/learn/08-events#overlays-modals-dialogs-toasts-and-menus))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Actions")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Button`, `IconButton`, `ButtonGroup`, `Dropdown`, `CopyButton`, `ThemeToggle`, `LocaleSwitch`, `Toggle`, `ToggleGroup`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Media")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Image`, `Video`, `Audio`, `Frame`, `Icon`, `Carousel` ([Add a picture](/recipes/add-a-picture), [Video and audio](/recipes/video-and-audio))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Typography")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Text`, `Heading`, `Paragraph`, `Code`, `Blockquote`, `Markdown`, `Unsafe.Html`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Somebody else's code")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Host`, `Element`, `Portal` ([Call your own JavaScript](/recipes/call-your-own-script), [Hand an element to a script](/recipes/hand-an-element-to-a-script))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Documents and decks")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Document`, `PageBreak`, `TableOfContents`, `Background`, `Watermark`, `Chart`, `QrCode`, `Presentation`, `Slide`, `TitleSlide`, `SectionSlide`, `TwoColumn`, `ImageSlide` ([PDF and slides](/learn/17-pdf-and-slides))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Routing")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Router` ([Pages](/learn/05-pages-and-routing#the-app-shell))") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Content: a note, a key, a figure", id: "content-a-note-a-key-a-figure").h3

        Markdown("The content built-ins are the pieces a page of text and figures is made of. Each is drawn the same on the live page, in the static paint and in a PDF:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Report(path: "/", title: "Report", description: "The month, at a glance.") {
    state tags = ["Rust", "Go"]
    Heading("Report").h1
    Callout("Figures are for the month to date.").note
    Row(gap: .lg) {
        Stat("Revenue", value: 48210, format: .currency, change: 0.124)
        Stat("Churn", value: 0.031, format: .percent, change: -0.02)
    }
    Text { Text("Search with ")  Kbd("mod+k") }
    for tag in tags by tag {
        Chip(tag).removable { on remove { tags = tags.filter(x => x != tag) } }
    }
    CodeBlock("wf build", language: "bash", title: "Terminal").copy
    Accordion.single {
        Accordion.Item("How is revenue counted?") { Text("Paid invoices, by the day they were paid.") }
        Accordion.Item("When does the month end?") { Text("At midnight UTC on its last day.") }
    }
    if tags.length == 0 {
        EmptyState(icon: "folder", title: "No tags", text: "Add one to group the report.")
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("- **`Callout`** is a note set apart from the text, with its kind's icon and word: `.note`, `.tip`, `.important`, `.warning`, `.caution`, or a `title:` of its own.\n- **`Stat`** says which way a figure moved, to a screen reader as well (\"Up 12.4%\").\n- **`Kbd`** writes a key combination as this system does: `⌘K` on a Mac, `Ctrl+K` elsewhere.\n- **`Chip`** can be pressed (`selected:`, a toggle button) and removed (`.removable`, its button named `Remove Rust`, firing `on remove`).\n- **`CodeBlock`** adds a bar with the file name and a copy button, and `.lines` numbers the lines.\n- **`Accordion`** is native `<details>`; `.single` keeps one open at a time.\n- **`VisuallyHidden`** is words only a screen reader hears.")

        Heading("What an element renders", id: "what-an-element-renders").h2

        Markdown("Each built-in renders real, semantic HTML. A `Button` is a `<button>`, a `Link` an `<a>`, a `Heading(…).h2` an `<h2>`, and a `Table` a `<table>` with `<th scope=\"col\">` cells in its head.\n\nEach also carries a stable class named after it, with a modifier class for each flag. For example, `Button(\"Save\").primary.lg` renders:")

        CodeBlock(#"<button class="wf-btn wf-btn--primary wf-btn--large" type="button">Save</button>"#, title: "html")

        Markdown("These classes are part of the public surface and stay stable across releases, so your own stylesheet may target them ([Styling](/learn/13-styling#classes-and-your-own-stylesheet)). The reference names each element's class.")

        Heading("What the compiler checks on every element", id: "what-the-compiler-checks-on-every-element").h2

        Markdown("The compiler holds every element call to these rules:")

        Table(caption: "What the compiler checks on every element") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("What it checks").header
                    Table.Cell("When it fails").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("The name is a built-in, a part inside its owner, or a declared component")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E101`; a part outside its owner is `C03`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("There is at most one positional argument, and the element takes one")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A second is `E113`; one a built-in does not take is `C05`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Every named prop exists, with a value of its type, and an enum prop's case exists")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A wrong type is `T01`; a missing case is `E103`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A named argument that is not a prop is a declared attribute")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Any other is written as an attribute, with `C04`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Every flag resolves to exactly one prop")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E103`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A handler names a DOM event, or one of the component's declared events")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V09` for any other name, such as a misspelled `on clik`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("A slot fill names a declared slot")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`E103`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("An `Icon(\"name\")` or `icon:` names an icon the runtime draws ([the list](/reference#icons))") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`V08`; the name shows as a word") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Accessibility: an `Image` has `alt:`, an `IconButton` a `label:`, a control a label, a `Modal` a title, a `Table` a `Table.Head`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`A01`–`A10`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Quick reference", id: "quick-reference").h2

        Table(caption: "Quick reference") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Syntax").header
                    Table.Cell("Meaning").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Name(value, prop: value)`") }
                    Table.Cell("An element with one positional and named props")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.flag`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A `Bool` prop set to `true`, or an enum prop's case") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`prop: .case`") }
                    Table.Cell("An enum prop, named")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\{ style \{ \} transition \{ \} on … \{ \} fills children \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("The block, in its fixed order")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`aria-label: \"…\"`, `data-tone: \"…\"`, `id: \"…\"`") }
                    Table.Cell("HTML attributes")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`Owner.Part \{ … \}`") }
                    Table.Cell("A part, inside its owner")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`class:`, `ref:`, `mount:`, `animate:`") }
                    Table.Cell("Universal props, on every element")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("The [components reference](/reference) lists each built-in's props, flags, events, slots and parts; `wf registry --json` prints the same data for a tool.")

        Heading("Common mistakes", id: "common-mistakes").h2

        Markdown("**A second positional argument.** Only the first argument may go without a name, so a tone passed after the label does not build:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Trash(path: "/", title: "Trash", description: "Empty the trash.") {
    Heading("Trash").h1
    Button("Empty the trash", "danger")
}"#, title: "wf · draws E113")

        Markdown("Write the tone as a flag, `Button(\"Empty the trash\").danger`, or name it, `tone: .danger` ([E113](/errors/E113)).\n\n**A flag the element does not have.** Flags are not a shared vocabulary; each element takes its own, and a word it does not declare stops the build:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Save(path: "/", title: "Save", description: "A save button.") {
    Heading("Save").h1
    Button("Save").huge
}"#, title: "wf · draws E103")

        Markdown("The finding lists the flags `Button` takes; `.lg` is its largest size ([E103](/errors/E103)).\n\n**A part outside its owner.** A part only means something inside the element it belongs to:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Profile(path: "/", title: "Profile", description: "A profile card.") {
    Heading("Profile").h1
    Card.Body { Text("Sam, designer") }
}"#, title: "wf · draws C03")

        Markdown("Wrap it in its owner, `Card \{ Card.Body \{ … \} \}`, or use a plain `Stack` ([C03](/errors/C03)).")
    }
}
